WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.905
Raw pointers are powerful because they
allow code to refer to memory without the

2
00:00:03.985 --> 00:00:05.611
normal reference checks of safe Rust.

3
00:00:05.691 --> 00:00:07.521
That power is why they need careful
review.

4
00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:10.700
The code must establish pointer validity,
alignment, initialization,

5
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.287
ownership, aliasing behavior, and lifetime
relationships through other means.

6
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:16.857
Review should ask whether the pointer
points to live data,

7
00:00:16.937 --> 00:00:19.224
whether the memory is correctly aligned
for the type,

8
00:00:19.304 --> 00:00:20.981
whether the data has been initialized,

9
00:00:21.061 --> 00:00:23.297
and whether the referenced memory outlives
the use.

10
00:00:23.377 --> 00:00:24.444
If mutation is involved,

11
00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:28.132
review must also ask whether the mutation
is exclusive when exclusivity is required

12
00:00:28.212 --> 00:00:29.076
by the abstraction.

13
00:00:29.156 --> 00:00:30.986
Aliasing assumptions should be explicit.

14
00:00:31.066 --> 00:00:33.810
Safe Rust references carry strict aliasing
and mutation rules.

15
00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.939
Raw pointer code may need to recreate
those guarantees through design.

16
00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:39.001
If multiple views can point to the same
memory,

17
00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:42.587
reviewers should understand which
operations are allowed, which are forbidden,

18
00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.649
and how the code prevents conflicting
access.

19
00:00:44.729 --> 00:00:47.829
Ownership transfer and cleanup
responsibility are equally important.

20
00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:50.145
If memory crosses a boundary, who owns it
afterward?

21
00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:50.784
Who frees it?

22
00:00:50.864 --> 00:00:52.084
Which allocator created it?

23
00:00:52.164 --> 00:00:53.536
Can the external code retain it?

24
00:00:53.616 --> 00:00:54.887
Can the Rust side use it again?

25
00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:58.575
These questions should be answered in the
API contract, not guessed from local code.

26
00:00:58.655 --> 00:01:02.212
Layout and representation assumptions
matter when code crosses FFI boundaries or

27
00:01:02.292 --> 00:01:03.918
interprets bytes as structured data.

28
00:01:03.998 --> 00:01:07.606
Defensive design keeps these assumptions
behind narrow abstractions and tests the

29
00:01:07.686 --> 00:01:08.550
boundary behavior.

30
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.155
The smaller the low-level surface,

31
00:01:10.235 --> 00:01:12.420
the easier it is to prove that the
assumptions hold.
